    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All tests pass (BUILD SUCCESS), and the acceptance suite's two new tests are green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the structured address form for owner creation. The change is spread across the layers where each piece of logic belongs:\n\n**API contract** (`openapi.yml`)\n- Added optional `addressLine1` (non-blank when present) and `addressLine2` properties to the `OwnerFields` schema.\n- Removed `address` from the `required` list so structured-only requests pass bean validation, while flat requests remain valid (backward compatible).\n\n**Domain model** (`Owner.java`, `schema.sql` \u00d7 4)\n- Added persisted `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns and accessors, plus matching `address_line1` / `address_line2` columns in the hsqldb (used by tests), h2, postgres and mysql schemas.\n\n**Address logic** (`AddressNormalizer.java`)\n- Added `compose(line1, line2)`, which returns the normalized `addressLine1` with a single space and normalized `addressLine2` appended when a second line is present \u2014 reusing the existing `normalize` rather than duplicating it.\n\n**Validation** (`OwnerFieldsValidator.java`)\n- The address requirement is now satisfied by either a non-blank `addressLine1` or the flat `address`; other mandatory fields (name, city, telephone) are unchanged.\n\n**Request handling** (`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`)\n- Replaced the single flat-address normalization with a small `resolveAddress` helper: it normalizes whichever address fields were supplied, drops blank structured lines to `null`, and sets the canonical stored `address` to the composed structured value when `addressLine1` is present, otherwise the normalized flat address.\n\nBecause the controller resolves the effective `address` before any downstream logic runs, everything that reads the address \u2014 the household hash, postcode validation and locality (which key off the resolved address/city/postcode) \u2014 automatically uses the structured fields when present and falls back to the flat address otherwise. The `address` returned in the response is the composed normalized string.",
